                <div class="mb-4 text-center">
                    <h3 class="mb-1 text-primary">Understanding Fundamental Analysis in Crypto</h3>
                    <img src="~/assets/images/Fundamental Analysis.jpeg" class="img-fluid my-3 rounded" alt="Crypto Fundamental Analysis" />
                </div>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">Introduction</h6>
                <p>
                    Fundamental analysis in crypto focuses on understanding why a project may deserve value beyond short-term hype. Instead of asking only where price might go next, FA asks whether a blockchain, protocol, or token has enough utility, adoption, and sustainability to matter over time.
                </p>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">1. What is Fundamental Analysis?</h6>
                <p>
                    Fundamental analysis helps traders and investors decide whether a crypto asset may be undervalued, overvalued, or simply too weak to justify attention. In crypto, this includes both traditional research and blockchain-native metrics such as token issuance, wallet growth, and network usage.
                </p>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">2. Key Factors to Consider</h6>
                <ul class="mb-4">
                    <li>
                        <strong>Project Whitepaper</strong><br />
                        A solid whitepaper explains the problem being solved, how the protocol works, where the token fits, and what the roadmap looks like. It should be clear, specific, and realistic.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Team & Advisors</strong><br />
                        A credible team with relevant experience and transparent communication can increase confidence. Anonymous teams are not always bad in crypto, but they increase risk and require extra caution.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Utility & Use Case</strong><br />
                        Ask what real function the product serves. Does the token have a meaningful role, or is it just attached to the project for speculation?
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Community & Developer Activity</strong><br />
                        Strong communities can help adoption, but meaningful developer activity matters more. Active development, shipping updates, and transparent changelogs are positive signals.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Tokenomics</strong><br />
                        Review total supply, circulating supply, emissions, vesting schedules, unlock events, burns, and concentration of ownership. Bad tokenomics can crush price even if the product is interesting.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>Partnerships & Adoption</strong><br />
                        Real usage is stronger than marketing. Look for actual integrations, ecosystem growth, volume, active users, and whether people genuinely need the protocol.
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">3. On-Chain Metrics</h6>
                <p>
                    Crypto gives investors access to data that is difficult to find in traditional markets. Useful on-chain metrics can include transaction count, active addresses, fees generated, validator participation, stablecoin inflows, and total value locked for DeFi protocols.
                </p>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">4. Crypto-Specific Areas to Evaluate</h6>
                <ul class="mb-4">
                    <li><strong>Token emissions:</strong> Is new supply entering the market aggressively?</li>
                    <li><strong>Unlock schedule:</strong> Are early investors or team tokens about to unlock?</li>
                    <li><strong>Revenue and fees:</strong> Does the protocol generate sustainable usage fees?</li>
                    <li><strong>Chain activity:</strong> Are users, developers, and liquidity growing or shrinking?</li>
                    <li><strong>Competitive position:</strong> Is the project actually differentiated or easily replaceable?</li>
                </ul>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">5. Red Flags to Watch Out For</h6>
                <ul class="mb-4">
                    <li>No product, no users, and no evidence of meaningful progress.</li>
                    <li>Inflated promises with vague roadmaps and weak documentation.</li>
                    <li>Heavy insider allocation or concentrated whale ownership.</li>
                    <li>Large upcoming token unlocks with little market demand.</li>
                    <li>Artificial community hype with no corresponding development quality.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="alert alert-info" role="alert">
                    <strong>Practical FA mindset:</strong> do not confuse popularity with quality. In crypto, narratives can push price far ahead of fundamentals for a while, but weak fundamentals often show up later through dilution, poor retention, or fading liquidity.
                </div>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">6. A Simple Fundamental Analysis Checklist</h6>
                <ul class="mb-4">
                    <li>What problem does this project solve?</li>
                    <li>Why does the token need to exist?</li>
                    <li>How is supply created, released, or locked?</li>
                    <li>Is the product being used in the real market?</li>
                    <li>Are users, fees, and developers increasing over time?</li>
                    <li>What could make the thesis fail?</li>
                </ul>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">Final Thoughts</h6>
                <p>
                    Charts can help with timing, but fundamentals help with selection. In crypto, the strongest research combines product understanding, tokenomics, on-chain evidence, and realistic expectations. Use FA to decide what deserves your attention before you decide where to enter.
                </p>
